# Workspace isolation

This project holds data for multiple separate contexts (workspaces) for
the same user, and later for multiple users per workspace. A bug here
means one workspace's data (e.g. job-related client data) leaking into
another (e.g. a student or ENK context) — treat this as a security
boundary, not a UX detail.

- Every table storing workspace-scoped data MUST have a `workspace_id`
  column and a Supabase Row Level Security policy restricting rows to
  workspaces the requesting `auth.uid()` is a member of, via
  `workspace_members`.
- Every API route or Server Action that reads or writes workspace-scoped
  data MUST re-verify the current user's membership in that workspace
  server-side. Never trust a `workspace_id` or workspace slug taken
  directly from a URL param, request body, or client-sent state without
  checking `workspace_members` against the authenticated user.
- When adding a new table, write its RLS policy in the same migration as
  the table itself — not as a follow-up task.
- When adding a new route under `apps/web/app/api/**` or a new page under
  `apps/web/app/w/[workspace]/**`, the first thing the handler does is
  resolve and verify workspace membership before touching any other data.
- If a query ever uses the Supabase service role key (which bypasses
  RLS), the membership check must happen in application code instead —
  do not assume RLS is protecting that query.
- Changes to files matching this rule should be validated with a second
  test account / second workspace to confirm no cross-workspace data is
  returned, not just tested against the one workspace you're actively
  working in.
